    description: "**Origin.** Filed 2026-05-30 as a sub-axis of WI-gogif following the linked-pile L3 investigation. Sub-axis C of the JSON-output-conventions umbrella.\n\n**The shape.** Two items describe wire-format vs. internal-API name/structure mismatches that consumers can't predict. The bm.json JSON key `pass` does not match the documented Python attribute `pass_id` (Python keyword workaround); the routes top-level field is documented in the schema but absent from bm.json (`hypergumbo routes` prints \"No API routes found\" because the field is missing, not because routes are absent).\n\n**Fix shape (different mechanic from A and B).** Generated wire-format schema doc — derive JSON-side keys + presence from the bm.json schema directly, not from prose. Plus a renaming policy for cases like `pass` → `pass_id` (either rename the JSON key, or document the alias explicitly in schema metadata). Different fix-site from \"missing flag\" or \"missing envelope\" — this is about the schema-to-JSON projection layer.\n\n**Members (2).** WI-nohoh (`AnalysisRun.pass` Python-keyword alias, `ir.py:275`), INV-pomol (routes top-level field absent from bm.json; partially self-superseded by pass-14 correction, only schema-organization observation + sketch-on-read side-finding remain).\n\n**Parent:** WI-gogif-nilul-vufuf-lakas-huzak-pigap-jutut-narun."  # 130d
    fields:  # 130d
      statement: "Every JSON-side key and presence must agree with the bm.json schema; renamed keys (Python-keyword aliases like pass→pass_id) must be documented as schema metadata not discovered by consumers."  # 130d
      root_cause: "Two faces of generated-schema-doc absence: AnalysisRun.pass Python-keyword workaround renames the JSON key without aliasing in schema (WI-nohoh, ir.py:275), routes top-level field documented but absent from bm.json (INV-pomol, partially self-superseded by pass-14 correction)."  # 130d
- op: discuss  # e637
  at: '2026-06-02T16:12:14Z'  # e637
  by: agent  # e637
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e637
  clock: 2  # e637
  nonce: e637  # e637
  message: "Pass-33 face: slice JSON envelope has 3 top-level keys vs bm.json's 22 — fundamentally different wire shape, both call themselves \"view\" (F33.2).\n\nINV-rugah's scope: \"Every JSON-side key and presence must agree with the bm.json schema; renamed keys must be documented as schema metadata not discovered by consumers.\" Pass-33 surfaces a wire-format-vs-bm.json asymmetry at the slice subcommand:\n\n**Slice JSON shape:**\n```\ntop-level keys: ['feature', 'schema_version', 'view']\nview = 'slice'\nfeature: { id, name, query, node_ids, edge_ids, entry_nodes,\n           node_depths, node_tiers, limits_hit }\n```\n\n**bm.json shape:**\n```\ntop-level keys: 22  — { schema_version, view, nodes, edges, entrypoints,\n                       features, usage_contexts, analysis_runs, metrics,\n                       profile, limits, supply_chain_summary,\n                       sketch_precomputed, validation_report,\n                       reproducibility_context, stable_id_scheme,\n                       shape_id_scheme, symbol_fingerprint_scheme,\n                       repo_fingerprint_scheme, confidence_model,\n                       generated_at, analysis_incomplete }\nview = 'behavior_map'\n```\n\nBoth JSON documents announce themselves via the `view` field — `'slice'` vs `'behavior_map'` — but the schema structure is otherwise disjoint:\n- bm.json: full record-oriented arrays at top level (`nodes`, `edges`)\n- slice JSON: ID-reference arrays under a `feature` envelope; full record data NOT included\n\n**Consumer impact.** A consumer reading both formats has to handle two distinct envelopes. The `view` field is the discriminator but isn't documented as one. Specifically:\n- `slice.feature.node_ids` is a list of substrate node IDs; full node records are NOT in slice output\n- Consumer must cross-reference against substrate (or substrate-derived index) to get node metadata\n- `slice.feature.edge_ids` similarly opaque\n- `slice.feature.node_depths` and `node_tiers` are dict {node_id: int} — a sparse projection useful only with substrate reference\n\nThis is correct design — slice's role is identifying a subgraph, not reproducing it — but the wire format gives the appearance of being a self-contained behavior map when it's a sparse index. Sub-pattern (1) \"same-fact-two-names\" at the `view` axis: bm.json says `view='behavior_map'` and ships records; slice says `view='slice'` and ships ID indexes. Same envelope-naming convention, two structurally-distinct payloads.\n\n**Cousin patterns:**\n- INV-rugah catalogued WI-nohoh (`pass`/`pass_id` Python-keyword workaround) — wire-format rename\n- INV-rugah catalogued INV-pomol (routes top-level field absent) — schema-vs-substrate presence gap\n- Pass-30 F30.7 (feature.summary documented but absent) — feature-record schema drift\n- Pass-32 F32.11 (`compact --help` says \"summary\" but key is `nodes_summary`) — help-text vs output-key\n\nPass-33's slice envelope finding is one level above these: not a field-name rename or a field absence, but a **fundamentally different document shape** under the same JSON-output API. Sub-axis of INV-rugah at the multi-view-envelope layer.\n\n**Fix-path candidate.** Per spec §13 (Output views), each subcommand's output schema should be formally documented with a JSON Schema. INV-rugah's \"generated wire-format schema doc\" suggestion applies — explicit schema for the slice view alongside the behavior_map view. Currently the only signal of \"which view am I reading\" is the `view` field; the structural contract is implicit.\n\n**No status change.** Pass-33 supplies a sub-axis to INV-rugah covering the multi-view-envelope wire shape. Probe artifact: `/tmp/p33_outputA3.log` section \"A.16-A.17\"."  # e637
